<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Project>

  <Import Project="Sdk.props" Sdk="Microsoft.DotNet.Arcade.Sdk" />

  <!-- Deliberately infrastructure-only.

       This file brings 17.3 onto Arcade's build/restore/CI plumbing and nothing else. It does not
       import main's policy: no analyzers, no LangVersion bump, no EnforceCodeStyleInBuild, no
       GenerateDocumentationFile, no TFM redefinitions. 17.3 keeps its own settings in
       scripts/build/TestPlatform.Settings.targets and its own package versions in
       scripts/build/TestPlatform.Dependencies.props. Old code, new infrastructure.

       Shape taken from the 16.11 servicing pipeline rather than derived, so the Era A
       servicing lines fail and pass in the same places. Every value below was re-measured on 17.3;
       where 17.3 differs from 16.11 the difference is called out. -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- 2022-era framework packages carry known CVEs. A servicing line cannot be made CVE-free. -->
    <NuGetAudit>false</NuGetAudit>

    <!-- NETSDK1215 - targeting netstandard < 2.0 is no longer recommended. True, not fixable here.
         NU1504     - a package declared twice with the same version. Pre-existing, harmless, and an
                      error only under current NuGet.

         NU1603 is deliberately NOT suppressed, and is promoted to an error explicitly rather than
         relying on TreatWarningsAsErrors, because the build runs with -warnAsError $false which
         would otherwise demote it back to a warning. A silent version substitution is exactly how
         a 17.x package ends up shipping content from a later band. -->
    <WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);NU1603</WarningsAsErrors>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);NETSDK1215;NU1504;NU1605;NU1701;NU1702</NoWarn>
    <!-- Servicing-age noise from building a 2021 line with a 2026 SDK. None of it is a product
         problem: NETSDK1138/1215 say the target frameworks are old, NETSDK1023 flags the MSTest
         reference this line pins on purpose, and CS1616/CS8105 are the signing key attribute
         duplicating CryptoKeyFile. MSB3276 is .NET Framework facade skew only (System.Runtime.*,
         System.Text.*, System.ValueTuple resolving from the SDK's net461 lib) - no vstest, MSTest
         or third-party product assembly is involved, so no version substitution is being hidden. -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);NETSDK1138;NETSDK1023;NETSDK1215;CS1616;CS8105</NoWarn>
    <MSBuildWarningsAsMessages>$(MSBuildWarningsAsMessages);MSB3276</MSBuildWarningsAsMessages>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- Arcade defaults DebugType to 'embedded'. This line shipped separate .pdb files, so embedding
       is artifact-affecting rather than cosmetic: it removes every .pdb from the packages and
       changes the assemblies that carry the debug data.

       This MUST be set in Directory.Build.props, never in .targets. The compiler honours a late
       override, but the decision to copy the .pdb to the output directory is made when the SDK
       targets are imported, which happens first. A late setting yields a portable assembly whose
       .pdb never leaves obj\ - indistinguishable from 'embedded' at the point where packaging
       fails, and it is scripts\build.ps1 that fails, several steps away from the cause. -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <DebugType>portable</DebugType>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- OUTPUT LAYOUT: left as Arcade's, deliberately. Do not add BaseOutputPath here.

       An earlier revision of this file set
         BaseOutputPath        = $(MSBuildProjectDirectory)\bin\
         BaseIntermediateOutputPath = $(MSBuildProjectDirectory)\obj\
       to keep the pre-Arcade src\<project>\bin\<Config>\<TFM> layout that scripts\build.ps1 and the
       hand-written nuspecs read from. MEASURED RESULT ON THIS BRANCH: it half-applied and produced a
       SPLIT layout, which is worse than either consistent option.

         compiled output  ->  artifacts\bin\<project>\<Config>\<TFM>\     (Arcade's, unchanged)
         intermediates    ->  test\<project>\obj\                          (moved)

       The reason is import order, not a typo. Arcade's Sdk.props is imported at the top of this
       file, and RepoLayout.props computes OutputPath from BaseOutputPath at that moment. Assigning
       BaseOutputPath afterwards changes a property whose consumer has already read it, so OutputPath
       keeps Arcade's value. BaseIntermediateOutputPath has a later consumer and did move. One
       assignment, two different outcomes, no diagnostic.

       16.11 carries the same block with a comment saying it preserves the layout. Its own pipeline
       shows the block is equally inert there: the version-split analysis in
       azure-pipelines-official.yml states that "$env:TP_OUT_DIR is <root>\artifacts, so TP_OUT_DIR\bin\
       is Arcade's output layout" and traces the copied files out of exactly that directory. So on
       both lines the build writes to Arcade's layout regardless of this block.

       The working answer is the one 16.11's Directory.Build.targets reached after reverting its OutDir
       override: leave the build output where Arcade puts it, and STAGE from Arcade's layout into the
       layout the nuspecs expect. Moving build output desynchronises every consumer that computes
       paths from Arcade's layout - project-to-project references and GenerateDepsFile both do - and
       it cannot fail on an incremental tree, so it survives local testing and breaks the first clean
       CI build. -->

  <!-- Self-contained publish of netcoreapp1.0 fails NETSDK1061 on a current SDK: restore resolves
       Microsoft.NETCore.App 1.0.16 while the SDK's table would publish 1.0.5. Pin both to the
       version restore already chooses, so nothing about the built output changes.
       17.3 has 4 netcoreapp1.0 projects, so this applies here as it does on 16.11. -->  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'netcoreapp1.0'">
    <RuntimeFrameworkVersion>1.0.16</RuntimeFrameworkVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- 17.3 ships 41 hand-written Properties\AssemblyInfo.cs files - twice 16.11's 20, measured on
       this branch. Arcade generates the same attributes, which is CS0579 on every one of them.
       TestPlatform.Settings.targets already turns off some; these are the ones Arcade adds on top. -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <GenerateAssemblyCopyrightAttribute>false</GenerateAssemblyCopyrightAttribute>
    <GenerateAssemblyCompanyAttribute>false</GenerateAssemblyCompanyAttribute>
    <GenerateAssemblyProductAttribute>false</GenerateAssemblyProductAttribute>
    <GenerateAssemblyConfigurationAttribute>false</GenerateAssemblyConfigurationAttribute>
    <GenerateNeutralResourcesLanguageAttribute>false</GenerateNeutralResourcesLanguageAttribute>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- Sign config. Arcade defaults StrongNameKeyId to its Open key. 17.3 signs with the Microsoft
       key: scripts\build\TestPlatform.Settings.targets sets SignAssembly=true with
       AssemblyOriginatorKeyFile=scripts\key.snk, and 156 InternalsVisibleTo declarations across
       src\ hard-code that same public key. Letting Arcade substitute its own key is CS0281 on
       every friend access, and would also produce a strong name that does not match the shipped
       artifact. -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <StrongNameKeyId>Microsoft</StrongNameKeyId>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
